 Please note that `disk_size_limit_bytes` defines the maximum size in bytes for the on-disk cache, while `memory_max_entries` specifies the maximum number of entries for the in-memory cache.
 
+## Expiring cache entries
+
+Pass a positive, finite number of seconds as `ttl` when configuring the cache:
+
+```python
+dspy.configure_cache(ttl=300)
+```
+
+Each successful write starts its own lifetime. Reading or promoting an entry
+from disk does not renew it, while overwriting it starts a new lifetime. Use
+`dspy.clients.set_ttl(seconds)` to change the lifetime for future writes without
+changing existing deadlines; `None` makes future entries nonexpiring. Expiry is
+shared by the memory and disk layers and persists when the disk cache is reopened.
+
 ## Understanding and Customizing the Cache
 
 In specific situations, you might want to implement a custom cache, for example, to gain finer control over how cache keys are generated. By default, the cache key is derived from a hash of all request arguments sent to `litellm`, excluding credentials like `api_key`.
